findings.body:
`True` is a Python keyword. In TypeScript it is an undefined identifier, so the success path throws a `ReferenceError`. The `catch` block swallows that error and returns `null`, so saving still works by accident. TypeScript will still report `Cannot find name 'True'`, which breaks type-checking.

The return value is also meaningless. `CodeEditor` calls `onChange(value || '')` and ignores the result (`workflow/nodes/_base/components/editor/code-editor/index.tsx:99-100`), so the function should not return anything.

::diff{file="web/app/components/app/configuration/config-var/config-modal/index.tsx" lines="130-144"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The PR author says `lint-staged` was run, but a `tsc` pass should catch this.

::suggestion{file="web/app/components/app/configuration/config-var/config-modal/index.tsx" lines="136-140"}
try {
  JSON.parse(value)
  handlePayloadChange('json_schema')(value)
}

`handleJSONSchemaChange` only stores the value when it parses as JSON. When the user types invalid JSON, the catch block returns without changing `tempPayload.json_schema`. The editor keeps showing the invalid text, because `jsonSchemaStr` is memoised on the stored value and does not change.

When the user clicks Save, `handleConfirm` validates the last valid schema, which passes, and calls `onConfirm` with that stale schema. There is no error. The user's edit is lost, and the `jsonSchemaInvalid` toast at line 320 can never fire from the UI.

Scenario: a schema is already saved. The user adds a trailing comma to the JSON and clicks Save. The old schema is stored and the modal closes without any message.

::diff{file="web/app/components/app/configuration/config-var/config-modal/index.tsx" lines="130-144"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The validation message `jsonSchemaInvalid` exists but is unreachable in this flow. Either keep the raw draft text so `handleConfirm` can validate it, or block the save.
